 rough-palo-alto/\nVenue: she learned the techniques of traditional paintin
 g. By the mid-1920s\, O’Keeffe w\nPrice: Free\nCategories: community\, f
 ree\nSource: funcheap/san-jose+funcheap/east-bay\n\nCelebrating the grand 
 opening of Hauser & Wirth Palo Alto and its inaugural exhibition\, ‘Cald
 er | O’Keeffe\,’ please join us during our first day-long program of t
 alks\, readings\, performances\, books\, music and food\, for an exhibitio
 n walkthrough with Georgia O’Keeffe Director of Curatorial Affairs Dr. L
 aura Wertheim Joseph and distinguished scholar and professor of American c
 ulture at Stanford University\, Alexander Nemerov to discuss the artist Ge
 orgia O’Keeffe and her exceptional works on view. Developed in close col
 laboration with the Calder Foundation\, ‘Calder | O’Keeffe’ brings t
 ogether works by Alexander Calder and Georgia O’Keeffe\, complemented by
  archival materials documenting their decades-long friendship. Georgia O
 ’Keeffe once remarked that she recognized her own ‘pelvis bone holes
 ’ in Calder’s forms—a striking moment of artistic recognition that s
 peaks to the unexpected links between their practices. By offering a fresh
  perspective on Calder and O’Keeffe’s little-known friendship\, this e
 xhibition takes its place alongside a series of presentations we’ve orga
 nized over the past 35 years\, aimed at revealing new and previously unexp
 lored pathways in the language of modernism
